paragraphs.text:
[38] Curiously enough, a provision which speaks directly to this issue is buried in the Immigration Regulations, 1978 (the "Regulations"). Subsection 5(2) of the Regulations, which defines who may sponsor another's application for permanent landing, contains the following clause:
(c) in the case of a permanent resident, the person is not subject to a removal order or a conditional removal order, other than a removal order that is of no effect because the person has, after the date of the order, been granted lawful permission to establish permanent residence in Canada;
c) dans le cas d'un résident permanent, il n'est pas visé par une mesure de renvoi ou une mesure de renvoi conditionnel, autre qu'une mesure de renvoi qui ne peut être exécutée en raison d'une admission légale ubséquente au Canada à titre de résident permanent;
It is eminently sensible that one who has acquired the right to stay in Canada cannot at the same time be subject to an order requiring him/her to leave Canada. However, this appears to be the only recognition of that fact in the Act or Regulations.
